{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"isolating a variable isolating a variable","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md","fileName":"Isolating a Variable.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Isolating a Variable","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Linear Equations and Modeling]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Equations and Modeling","Working Backward","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"isolating a variable isolating a variable","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md","fileName":"Isolating a Variable.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Isolating a Variable","heading":"Isolating a Variable","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Isolating a variable rewrites an equation so the target variable appears alone on one side. The process reverses surrounding operations in an order that preserves equality.\n\nFor (3x-5=28), add five to both sides to obtain (3x=33), then divide both sides by three to obtain (x=11). Expressions may first need to be expanded, like terms combined, or denominators cleared. Isolation is an algebraic form of [[Working Backward]]: undo the operations applied to the unknown while applying each change to both sides.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Equations and Modeling","Working Backward","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Isolating a Variable.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__non